{"tool": "answer", "text": "A process that is not root cannot listen on port 80 because port 80 is a well-known port (below 1024), and by default, only the root user can bind to such ports. This restriction ensures that services on well-known ports are trusted and not subject to arbitrary user-level modifications.

To allow a non-root process to listen on port 80, the system administrator must explicitly configure the system to permit it. This typically involves modifying the system's configuration (e.g., in /etc/services or /etc/inetd.conf) and ensuring that the necessary permissions are granted. However, this is generally discouraged due to security risks.

Alternatively, the process can bind to a higher-numbered port (e.g., 8080), which is not restricted to root. This is the standard and recommended practice for web servers or services that do not require the well-known port.", "cite": 2}
